  "statement": "(i) Prove the identity $\\cos 2\\theta = 1-2\\sin^{2}\\theta$. (ii) Hence solve $2\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos\\theta=1$ for $0\\le\\theta\\le 2\\pi$. (iii) Write down the general solution.",
  "solution": "(i) $\\cos 2\\theta=\\cos^{2}\\theta-\\sin^{2}\\theta=1-\\sin^{2}\\theta-\\sin^{2}\\theta=1-2\\sin^{2}\\theta$. (ii) $2\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos\\theta=1\\Rightarrow 2(1-\\cos^{2}\\theta)+\\cos\\theta=1\\Rightarrow 2\\cos^{2}\\theta-\\cos\\theta-1=0\\Rightarrow (2\\cos\\theta+1)(\\cos\\theta-1)=0$. So $\\cos\\theta=1$ or $-\\tfrac12$: $\\theta=0,2\\pi,\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}$. (iii) General: $\\theta=2k\\pi$ or $\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}+2k\\pi$, $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.",
  "solutionStatus": "full",
  "hints": [
    "Start from cos^2 - sin^2.",
    "Substitute to get a quadratic in cos theta."
  ],
  "_difficultyNote": "[难度自评: Q1否 Q2是 Q3否 Q4是 Q5是 → 4星]"
